<p>
  DynShift is an independent software studio. It makes a small number of
  focused tools, publishes most of them as open source, and ships each one
  finished. It is run by its founder, who designs and builds the products,
  writes the documentation and answers the issues. This article is about why it
  is organised the way it is, and what that means for the people who use what it
  makes.
</p>

<h2>What exists so far</h2>
<p>
  Two kinds of software have come out of the studio. The first is
  <strong>products</strong>, things a person installs and uses.
  <a href="/projects/phonepad/">PhonePad</a>, which turns an Android phone into a
  wireless controller for a Windows PC, is the first to reach a public release.
  The second is <strong>developer tools</strong>:
  <a href="/projects/layr/">LAYR</a>, a compiled language for web layout, and four
  <a href="/packages/">Flutter packages</a> on pub.dev, each written to remove one
  recurring problem from app work. A private social app, Privogram, is in
  development and has not been released.
</p>

