<h2>One thing at a time</h2>
<p>
  The studio works on one project until it is done, releases it, and then starts
  the next. There is no portfolio of half-built ideas competing for attention.
  That sounds like a constraint, and it is one, chosen on purpose: a tool that is
  nearly finished helps nobody, and attention split across five unfinished
  things finishes none of them.
</p>
<p>
  It also explains why the site lists so few projects. Something appears on
  dynshift.com when it ships, not when it is announced.
</p>

<h2>What "finished" means</h2>
<p>
  "Finished" is easy to say and easy to stretch, so the studio defines it. A tool
  leaves when three things are true.
</p>

<h3>It does the whole job it set out to do</h3>
<p>
  A controller that cannot rumble is not quite the controller it claims to be.
  Rumble was at one point listed among PhonePad's limitations; it was made to
  work before the first release, by reading the vibration request from the
  virtual-controller driver as a notification and forwarding it to the phone's
  motor, and verified end to end on a real phone.
</p>

<h3>It has been measured, not assumed to work</h3>
<p>
  Every performance figure quoted for a DynShift product comes from a recorded
  run, with the method written next to the result. PhonePad's documentation goes
  further and keeps a list of what has not been measured yet, so a reader can tell
  the difference between "fast" and "measured to be fast". When a measurement
  turns out to have been wrong, as one of PhonePad's packet-rate figures was, the
  correction is published in the same place, with the explanation of what went
  wrong left beside it.
</p>

<h3>Someone else could maintain it</h3>
<p>
  Code is written to be read. Comments explain why rather than what, and the
  failure that shaped a piece of code is recorded in the code that fixed it.
  LAYR's documentation compiles every one of its examples in continuous
  integration, so the docs cannot quietly drift from the language. This is also
  why so much of the work is public: source that strangers will read gets written
  more carefully than source nobody will see.
</p>

<h2>Principles that show up in the products</h2>
<p>
  A few convictions recur across the projects, and they are easier to see in
  what the products refuse to do than in anything the studio says about itself.
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Your data stays with you.</strong> PhonePad has no account, no server
    and no telemetry; the phone talks to your PC on your network and to nothing
    else. LAYR sites are built as static files that can be hosted anywhere.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>No paywall on what works.</strong> Everything released so far
    is free, and the free versions are the products, not demos of them. If paid
    extras ever appear, they will be additions, never a charge for what already
    works.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Honest limits.</strong> Each project documents what it does not do.
    PhonePad will not ship on iOS, because there is no way to do it as well as on
    Android, and doing it badly would be worse than not doing it.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Borrow infrastructure, own the meaning.</strong> LAYR renders through
    React and lets the browser's own engine do the layout; PhonePad stands on a
    proven virtual-controller driver rather than writing a kernel driver of its
    own. The studio spends its effort where its tools say something new.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2>Open source, and which licence</h2>
<p>
  The licence follows the kind of software. Products are published under the
  <strong>GNU GPL-3.0</strong>: anyone can read, build, change and redistribute
  them, and anything derived from them must stay open on the same terms.
  Libraries are published under the <strong>MIT licence</strong>, because a
  library is only useful if it can be used inside anything, including closed
  software. Where a product defines a format others might want to implement,
  such as PhonePad's wire protocol, that part is published under Apache-2.0, so
  a compatible client does not inherit the GPL.
</p>
<p>
  Public source lives under <a href="https://github.com/MNBLabs" rel="noopener">MNBLabs</a>
  on GitHub, with issues and discussions open on every public repository. The
  names DynShift and PhonePad are unregistered trademarks; forks are welcome,
  under their own names.
</p>

<h2>How it is sustained</h2>
<p>
  People who find a tool useful can support the time behind it through
  <a href="https://github.com/sponsors/MNBLabs" rel="noopener">GitHub Sponsors</a>
  or <a href="https://ko-fi.com/nishanbhuinya" rel="noopener">Ko-fi</a>. Support
  does not unlock anything; every user gets the same software.
</p>

<h2>Where it is going</h2>
<p>
  The first project to find an audience on its own is PhonePad: in its first
  eight weeks it was downloaded from GitHub more than 900 times, without any
  promotion, and its users have begun reporting the cases it does not cover yet
  and proposing fixes for them.
</p>
<p>
  Software is the starting point, not the boundary. The intention is to grow
  from tools into games, and further out into hardware. None of that has a date,
  and none of it will be announced before it exists: on this site, a project
  appears when it ships.
</p>
<p>
  In the meantime, the articles here explain the work in more depth than a
  product page can: how the projects are built, what was measured, and what was
  learned along the way. <a href="/articles/introducing-phonepad/">Introducing
  PhonePad</a> and <a href="/articles/introducing-layr/">Introducing LAYR</a> are
  good places to start.
</p>
